  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A pleasant day out across ENC today with highs\nin the 60s to low 70s and light NE'rly winds as the area \nremains under control of high pressure. High cirrus continues to\nstream in from the south from a stalled surface trough remains \nto our south. As we get into tonight expect winds to become\nlight and variable to calm though with high cirrus streaming\nover the area tonight dont expect a fog threat tonight. Lows get\ndown into the 30s/40s inland and 50s along the OBX. Another warm\nday is forecast across the area on Fri with highs getting into\nthe 60s/70s.  \n\nWe will remain dry with high pressure remaining overhead tonight\nand tomorrow. High then pushes off the coast by Saturday. Fire \nweather concerns may remain through at least Friday with the \ncuring vegetation combined with low RH values each afternoon. \nLuckily winds will not be an issue with a light gradient in \nplace. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...No significant changes since the previous update\nas while there is high confidence in a low developing off the\nSE coast, there remains low confidence in potential impacts to \nthe area. Low develops off the FL coast Fri/Sat and gains \nstrength in response to a shortwave digging across the Gulf \nabove surface coastal troughing ongoing along the FL leg of the\nGulfstream. However, what happens once a pressure center \npresents itself remains fairly uncertain with the typical \nquestions for a low that hasn't even formed yet off the coast: \nwhere the system will track, how much it deepens, how fast is it\nmoving, etc... What structure/characteristics the low will have\nis also in question with NHC offering up 60% chance that this\nlow could take on subtropical, and maybe even tropical\ncharacteristics. The key point here is: the impacts will not\nchange based on what this low ends up being called. Initially a\ngenerally Nward motion is expected. Day time models have show a\nslightly weaker and westward trend, but still keep a wide swath\nof solutions, with some back and forth between possible\nsolutions even within the same model. A majority of ensemble\nguidance continues to suggest the center of the low moves\nthrough between the NC/VA border and ~40 miles offshore of Cape\nHatteras. Details will become clearer over the next 24hrs or so\nas we get further into the HiRes window, get more data on the \nupper level pattern progression, see how convection over the \nGulf and Sern Gulfstream plays out, and get closer to actual \ncyclogenesis. Bottom line even though we are a day closer, \nnothing has been ironed out! \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...King Tide Cycle starts second half of this \nweekend, peaking middle of next week. This higher than normal \nastronomical tide cycle is slightly stronger than the one that \noccurred the last week of OCT. Will have to see how the system \nin Key Message 2 and the potential wind increase in Key Message\n4 play out before we can determine what sort of effect this \nKing Tide cycle will have on our coast.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 4...In the wake of this weekend's low, a high\nbriefly builds in early next week. Mid-week, models indicate a  \ndeepening trough moving through the Gulf states and progressing\noffshore of the SE US. This shortwave may encounter a stationary\nboundary offshore of SE US and the baroclinic zone along the\nGulf Stream and produce a sfc short wave or low. The pressure\ngradient would tighten considerably in this scenario, with the\nhigh sitting over the NE. Confidence remains low at this point,\nbut if materialized, a period of higher wind and seas is \npossible, coinciding with the peak of King Tides (See Key \nMessage 3). We will have to see how this weekend's low plays \nout, until then the confidence will remain low for the forecast \nmid next week.",
